.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt AZRAMCTL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm azramctl
.Nd control the compressed cache in front of swap
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Cm status
.Nm
.Cm hist
.Nm
.Cm enable | disable
.Nm
.Cm measure Cm on | off
.Nm
.Cm set Cm pool_max Ar size Ns Op Cm %
.Nm
.Cm set Cm reject_size Ar bytes
.Nm
.Cm reset
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
controls
.Xr azram 4
and shows what it holds and what it has done.
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm status
Show the state and the settings; what
.Xr azram 4
holds now, the memory that takes and the memory it saves; and the
counters: the pages offered on their way to swap, how many were kept and
how many declined for each reason, the pages paged in from
.Xr azram 4 ,
the time to compress and decompress a page, and the pages written to and
read from the swap device, with the time a read took on average.
This is the default command.
.Pp
The compression ratio is the size of the pages kept compressed over the
size of their compressed data.
The pool holds more memory than the data, since each page takes its size
class and freed pages leave holes in their chunks; the status shows how
many times the data it holds.
The memory saved is the size of the pages held less the memory of the pool
and of the metadata.
.It Cm hist
Show the compressed sizes of the pages looked at on their way to swap, in
steps of 1/64 page, and what share of the pages each value of
.Va vm.azram.reject_size
from 1/2 to 7/8 page would keep, with the compression ratio of the pages
it would keep.
Pages of one byte value cost nothing and count as kept.
.It Cm enable | disable
Set or clear
.Va vm.azram.enable .
.It Cm measure Cm on | off
Set or clear
.Va vm.azram.measure :
while
.Xr azram 4
is disabled, compress the pages that go to swap only to count their sizes.
.It Cm set Cm pool_max Ar size Ns Op Cm %
Set the memory the compressed pages may take, in bytes with an optional
k, m or g suffix, or as a percentage of physical memory.
.It Cm set Cm reject_size Ar bytes
Set the largest compressed size of a page that is kept.
.It Cm reset
Reset the counters, so that
.Cm status
and
.Cm hist
cover what happens from now on.
.El
.Pp
Only root may change the settings or reset the counters.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Estimate what
.Xr azram 4
would save on a workload, without changing how the system pages:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# azramctl disable
# azramctl measure on
# azramctl reset
\&... run the workload ...
# azramctl hist
.Ed
.Pp
Compare the reads from the swap device and the time they took with
.Xr azram 4
on and off:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# azramctl reset
\&... run the workload ...
# azramctl status
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr azram 4 ,
.Xr swapctl 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
was written for AutumnBSD.
